---
import FlowShot from '../FlowShot.astro';
import FlowDetail from '../FlowDetail.astro';
import FlowShot from '../../../components/flow/FlowShot.astro';
import FlowDetail from '../../../components/flow/FlowDetail.astro';
import shotToolbar from '../../../assets/flow/shots/flow-editor-toolbar-shot.webp';
import detailToolbarBlocked from '../../../assets/flow/details/detail-toolbar-blocked.webp';
import detailToolbarArmed from '../../../assets/flow/details/detail-toolbar-armed.webp';
import detailToolbarMenu from '../../../assets/flow/details/detail-toolbar-menu.webp';
---
{/* Chapter 1b: the tool strip (2026-08-18, from the editor-toolbar brief).
    Sits directly after Agency because it is the same five actions seen from
    the other end — Agency is what happens after you commit, this is what you
    are told beforehand and where the tools actually live.

    STAGE CORNER: --tl. The next stage down the page (Long documents) takes
    --br, so adjacent bands do not repeat a corner; tests/e2e/product-stage
    asserts exactly that. */}
<section class="border-t border-border/60 px-6 py-14 sm:py-20" aria-labelledby="tour-toolbar">
  <div>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
      <p class="of-eyebrow" data-animate="rise">The tool strip</p>
      <h3 id="tour-toolbar" class="of-display scroll-mt-28 mt-4 text-[clamp(1.85rem,3.4vw,2.75rem)]" data-animate="lead">The tools come to the document.</h3>
      <p class="of-lede mt-5" data-animate="rise">
        The strip lives in the document's top-right corner and stays out of the way until you
        reach for it. Your writing tools sit on one side of a divider and the five AI actions
        on the other, because both act on the same document. The AI ones are marked with
        letters rather than icons: five text operations do not have five icons you could tell
        apart, and the same marks appear in the Agency menu so the two never drift.
      </p>
      <p class="of-lede mt-4" data-animate="rise">
        When an action cannot run, it stays where it is, greyed, and the same panel says why in
        words that came from whatever refused it. In a document that already has a table,
        Convert Text to Table reads "Already contains a table". In one with nothing to convert,
        the same tool gives a different reason. The scope line stays, because it is still true;
        the cost and the model do not, because a run that cannot happen has neither.
      </p>
    </div>

    {/* The armed strip, at rest and full width. Both crops on the stage
        below carry a popover across half the strip; this is the only cut
        where the DIVIDER is visible end to end, which is the chapter's
        actual claim. Sized narrow and centred so it reads as one object
        rather than a band. */}
    <figure class="toolbar-armed mx-auto mt-12" data-animate="rise">
      <FlowDetail
        src={detailToolbarArmed}
        alt="Flow's document tool strip at rest: bold, italic, text size and alignment controls, a divider, then five AI marks. Pr, Sm, Tr and Tt are in colour and ready; Tx is greyed out."
        caption="Four ready, one unavailable · The divider is the whole idea"
        sizes="(min-width: 900px) 34rem, calc(100vw - 3rem)"
      />
    </figure>
    <p class="toolbar-armed__note mx-auto mt-5 text-center" data-animate="rise">
      A tool you can run is in full colour. A tool you cannot is still there, greyed, rather
      than hidden, so the set never changes shape under you.
    </p>
    <div class="of-stage of-stage--pad-top mt-12" data-animate="plate">
      <FlowShot
        src={shotToolbar}
        alt="Orionfold Flow with the document tool strip expanded over an annual operating review: writing controls, a divider, and five colored AI monograms, with Proofread's hover panel stating the scope, cost, and model for the run."
        crop
        sizes="(min-width: 1280px) 78rem, (min-width: 1024px) 64rem, 100vw"
      />
      <FlowDetail
        class="of-stage__detail of-stage__detail--tl"
        src={detailToolbarBlocked}
        alt="Convert Text to Table greyed out in Flow's tool strip, its panel reading 'Unavailable, already contains a table' above the surviving scope line of 343 words for the whole document."
        caption="A refusal names its own reason · The scope line survives, the cost does not"
        sizes="(min-width: 1280px) 30rem, (min-width: 1024px) 27rem, calc(100vw - 3rem)"
        animate
      />
    </div>

    {/* The same five actions in the macOS menu bar. This is a DIFFERENT
        claim from the strip and needs its own picture: the monograms are
        identical across both surfaces (the brief's "cannot drift"), the
        keyboard route exists, and the two actions that take a parameter are
        the two carrying a submenu arrow. Paired with a short copy column
        rather than given a full band — it is a corroborating detail, not a
        chapter of its own. */}
    <div class="toolbar-menu mx-auto mt-16">
      <div class="toolbar-menu__copy" data-animate="rise">
        <h4 class="toolbar-menu__head">The same five, wherever you reach for them.</h4>
        <p class="toolbar-menu__body">
          Every action in the strip is also in the menu bar, carrying the same mark, so nothing
          has to be relearned between the two. Proofread has a keyboard shortcut. Two actions
          need an answer before they can run: Translate, and turning a table back into text.
          Those two show an arrow, because they open their choices rather than starting.
        </p>
      </div>
      <FlowDetail
        src={detailToolbarMenu}
        alt="Flow's Agency menu open in the macOS menu bar: Proofread with the shift-command-P shortcut, Summarize, Translate with a submenu arrow, Convert Text to Table, and Convert Table to Text with a submenu arrow, each beside the same coloured mark used in the document's tool strip."
        caption="One shortcut, two submenus · The marks match the strip exactly"
        sizes="(min-width: 900px) 26rem, calc(100vw - 3rem)"
      />
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
